# trym3 — CTF Tools Suite

A Python-based CLI tool suite for solving CTF (Capture The Flag) challenges
across 3 main categories: **Crypto**, **Forensics**, and **Pwn**.

```
 _                        _____
| |_ _ __ _   _ _ __ ___ |___ /
| __| '__| | | | '_ ` _ \  |_ \
| |_| |  | |_| | | | | | |___) |
 \__|_|   \__, |_| |_| |_|____/
          |___/
```

## Tools

| Tool | Category | Description |
|------|----------|-------------|
| `autodecode` | Crypto | Multi-cipher auto-decoder (base64, hex, rot13, morse, etc.) with chaining & confidence scoring |
| `rsatool` | Crypto | RSA attack helper (common factor, low exponent, wiener, fermat) |
| `fscan` | Forensics | File signature & metadata scanner (magic bytes vs extension, hidden files) |
| `offsetfind` | Pwn | Buffer overflow offset finder (cyclic pattern + checksec) |

## Installation

> **Note:** `offsetfind` needs **pwntools**, which recently got native wheels
> (`unicorn`, `capstone`) for Python 3.13. Use `python3.13` for a single venv
> that can run everything.

```bash
make setup          # create .venv + install every tool + ctftools
```

Or manually:

```bash
python3.13 -m venv .venv
source .venv/bin/activate
pip install -e ./autodecode -e ./fscan -e ./rsatool -e ./offsetfind -e .
```

After that all commands are available directly: `autodecode`, `fscan`,
`rsatool`, `offsetfind`, plus the `ctftools` launcher. Each tool also ships
its own `requirements.txt` if you want to install them separately.

Verify everything works with:

```bash
make test
```

> **Note:** the project folder contains directories with the same names
> (`autodecode/`, etc.), so do not `import autodecode` from the repo root
> (it will shadow the project folder instead of the installed package). Use
> the console scripts or `ctftools <tool>`.

## Unified Entry Point

Once the tools are installed, use `ctftools` as a single launcher (it
dispatches to the console scripts via `execv`, so exit codes & streams are
inherited):

```bash
ctftools --help
ctftools autodecode "SGVsbG8gQ1RGIQ=="
ctftools rsatool --n 12345 --e 3 --c 98765
ctftools fscan ./folder --recursive --output json
ctftools offsetfind ./vuln_binary
```

### Interactive menu

Run `ctftools` without a subcommand to pick a tool interactively. The trym3
ASCII banner is shown first, then a numbered list:

```text
 _                        _____
| |_ _ __ _   _ _ __ ___ |___ /
| __| '__| | | | '_ ` _ \  |_ \
| |_| |  | |_| | | | | | |___) |
 \__|_|   \__, |_| |_| |_|____/
          |___/
trym3 - CTF Tools Launcher

Select a tool:

  1. autodecode
  2. fscan
  3. rsatool
  4. offsetfind
  0. Exit

Choice [0-4]:
```

Type a number, then the launcher shows that tool's `--help`. The list is read
from the `name.txt` file at the repo root (one tool name per line), so edit
that file to add/remove tools shown in the menu.

## Tutorials

### autodecode — Multi-Cipher Auto-Decoder

Decode a single or chained encoding automatically, ranked by a confidence
score.

```bash
# Basic decode
autodecode "SGVsbG8gQ1RGIQ=="

# Only show highly-readable results, deepen the chaining search
autodecode --only-readable --max-depth 3 "VGhpcyBpcyBhIHRlc3Q="

# Pipe input from another command
echo "..." | autodecode

# Exhaustive BFS over every candidate
autodecode --all "SGVsbG8="
```

Key options:

| Option | Description |
|--------|-------------|
| `TEXT` | Ciphertext as a positional argument |
| `--only-readable` | Show only high-confidence results |
| `--max-depth N` | Maximum chaining iterations (default 5) |
| `--min-score F` | Confidence threshold 0.0–1.0 |
| `--no-color` | Disable colored output |

Supported methods: Base64, Base32, Base16/Hex, ROT13, ROT47, Morse Code,
Binary, URL Encoding, HTML entities, and Caesar cipher (all 25 shifts).

### rsatool — RSA Attack Helper

```bash
# Single key set (decimal)
rsatool --n 12345... --e 3 --c 98765...

# Values as hex
rsatool --n 0x... --e 0x10001 --c 0x... --hex

# Run every attack even after one succeeds
rsatool --input keys.json --all

# Run only the Wiener attack
rsatool --input keys.json --attack wiener

# Run several specific attacks
rsatool --n 12345 --e 11 --c 9999 --attack common_factor,low_exp
```

Key options:

| Option | Description |
|--------|-------------|
| `--n`, `--e`, `--c` | Single RSA parameters (decimal, or hex with `--hex`) |
| `--input FILE` | JSON file with key sets (multiple allowed for common factor) |
| `--attack NAME` | Run only specific attacks (comma-separated for several) |
| `--all` | Run all attacks even after one succeeds |
| `--timeout SEC` | Per-attack timeout (default 60) |
| `--hex` | Interpret `--n/--e/--c` as hex |

JSON key file format:

```json
{
  "keys": [
    {"n": "221...", "e": 3, "c": "123..."},
    {"n": "...", "e": 65537, "c": "..."}
  ]
}
```

Values can be decimal strings/ints or hex strings (with a `0x` prefix).

Supported attacks:

- `common_factor`: Common Factor (GCD across multiple `n`)
- `low_exp`: Low Public Exponent (small `e`, `m^e < n`, cube root for `e=3`)
- `wiener`: Wiener's Attack (small `d`, continued fractions)
- `fermat`: Fermat Factorization (close `p`, `q`)

### fscan — File Signature & Metadata Scanner

```bash
# Inspect a single file
fscan suspicious.jpg

# Scan a folder recursively
fscan ./forensics_folder --recursive

# Extract detected hidden files
fscan hidden.png --extract

# JSON output
fscan ./folder --output json
```

Key options:

| Option | Description |
|--------|-------------|
| `PATH` | Single file or folder |
| `--recursive` | Scan folders recursively |
| `--output {table,json}` | Output format (default table) |
| `--extract` | Extract detected hidden files to `./<file>_extracted/` |
| `--strings-min MIN` | Minimum length for embedded strings (default 6) |
| `--no-strings` | Disable string extraction |

Supported magic-byte formats: PNG, JPEG, GIF, BMP, WEBP, PDF, ZIP/OOXML,
RAR, 7z, gzip, bzip2, xz, tar, ELF, PE, Mach-O, RIFF/WAV/AVI, FLAC, MP3, OGG,
MP4, SQLite, Java class, PSD, ICO, TIFF, MIDI, PCAP(NG), OLE/doc.

### offsetfind — Pwn Buffer Overflow Offset Finder

```bash
# Basic local binary
offsetfind ./vuln_binary

# Probe a remote service (offset computed from the local binary)
offsetfind ./vuln_binary --host target.ctf.com --port 1337

# Bigger cyclic pattern
offsetfind ./vuln_binary --pattern-length 2048

# Only print the offset value (for scripting)
offsetfind ./vuln_binary --offset-only
```

Key options:

| Option | Description |
|--------|-------------|
| `BINARY` | Path to the local binary |
| `--host`, `--port` | Remote target service |
| `--pattern-length N` | Cyclic pattern length (default 1024) |
| `--timeout SEC` | Process/connection timeout (default 5) |
| `--no-checksec` | Skip the checksec protection overview |
| `--offset-only` | Only print the offset value (compact output) |

Example output for a local binary (offset fully computed from the crash plus
core dump, including the base address for PIE binaries):

```text
Buffer overflow offset: 40

Fault address : 0x6161616161616166
RIP           : 0x55b4187e61a0
RSP           : 0x7ffed4d43b78

Base address (PIE) : 0x55b4187e5000
```

For a remote target, the offset is still computed from the local binary (if
given); the remote connection is only used to confirm the payload crashes the
service (it drops the connection). Without a local binary, `offsetfind` can
only report the remote status, not the offset value.

## Ethical Notes

All tools are intended for legal/educational CTF activities (official
challenges, private labs). Do not use them to attack systems without
authorization.

## Repo Structure

```
cli/
├── ctftools/             # Unified launcher (entry point `ctftools`, trym3 banner)
├── autodecode/           # Tool 1 — crypto auto-decode
├── fscan/                # Tool 2 — file signature scanner
├── rsatool/              # Tool 3 — RSA attacks
├── offsetfind/           # Tool 4 — buffer overflow offset finder
├── docs/PRD_CTF_Tools.md
├── pyproject.toml
├── name.txt              # Tool list for the ctftools menu
└── README.md
```



